VT_TALK("sql/sql-normalization", {
 "anomaly": "Normalization is the method for splitting tables so that every fact is stored once. Look at the teacher's enrolment sheet. Asha's name is stored twice, and teacher Rao's room is stored three times. If Rao moves and someone fixes only one row, the sheet now says Rao sits in two rooms at once. That is an update anomaly. There are two more. A new course with no students cannot be recorded at all, and if the only student of a course leaves, the course disappears with her row. Normalization fixes all three by giving each kind of fact its own table. Think of a school that keeps one master list of teachers, instead of writing the staff room on every class register.",
 "fd": "A functional dependency says that once you know one thing, another thing is decided. Knowing a roll number tells you the student's name, and knowing a teacher tells you their room. We write this with an arrow, roll number arrow name. The key of the enrolment sheet is the pair of student and course, because together they decide every other column. Problems appear when a column depends on only part of the key, like the name depending on the student alone, or on a column that is not a key at all, like the room depending on the teacher. You can test a dependency by grouping on the left side and counting distinct values on the right side. Think of how a pin code decides the post office.",
 "forms": "Each normal form removes one kind of bad dependency. First normal form says every cell holds one value, so a list of phone numbers becomes one row per phone. Second normal form says no column may depend on only part of the key, so the student's name moves to a students table. Third normal form says no column may depend on another column that is not a key, so the room moves to a teachers table. Boyce Codd normal form is a slightly stricter third form. In the animation, choose how far to go and watch the sheet split, and at the end a join rebuilds the original rows, so nothing is lost. A popular memory line says every column depends on the key, the whole key, and nothing but the key.",
 "denorm": "Most business databases stop at third normal form. Fourth and fifth normal forms exist for rarer cases, such as two independent lists squeezed into one table. Denormalization is the opposite move, made on purpose. You store a copy of some data, such as an item count on each order, so that reading is faster, and you accept the extra work of keeping the copy correct. Do it only after normalizing, and only when a measured query is too slow even with good indexes. Reporting databases use it a lot, because their data is loaded in batches and rarely edited. Think of a shop that keeps a running total on the till instead of adding every receipt again.",
 "levels": "Let us climb the ladder. For a beginner, store each fact once, and if you would have to fix something in two places, split the table. At college level, find the key and the dependencies, remove partial ones for second normal form and transitive ones for third, and check that a join rebuilds the rows. At masters level, a split is lossless when the shared columns form a key of one part, and Armstrong's axioms derive every dependency that follows from the ones you know. At research level, algorithms discover dependencies from data automatically. Think of a detective who works out who is related to whom from a family album."
});
